(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["chem-12-15"] = {"15.1":{"s":"Polymers are very large molecules made by joining many small units called monomers. Plastics, synthetic fibres and rubbers are all polymers and are part of daily life. This chapter covers how polymers are classified, some important ones, their molecular mass and biodegradable polymers."},"15.2":{"s":"Polymers are classified in several ways. By source: natural, semi-synthetic or synthetic. By structure: linear, branched or cross-linked. By how they form: addition, condensation or ring-opening. By forces: elastomers, fibres, thermoplastics, thermosets. By monomers: homopolymers or copolymers.","w":["Addition polymers lose nothing as they form; condensation polymers lose a small molecule such as water at each step."]},"15.3":{"s":"Some important polymers. Natural rubber is a polymer of isoprene; vulcanising it with sulfur makes it harder and more elastic. Others include polythene, PVC, Teflon, nylon-6,6 and nylon-6, terylene (a polyester), Bakelite (phenol-formaldehyde) and Buna-S and Buna-N rubbers.","w":["Monomers: polythene from ethene, PVC from vinyl chloride, Teflon from tetrafluoroethene."]},"15.4":{"s":"A polymer is a mixture of molecules of different sizes, so its molecular mass is an average. The degree of polymerisation (DP) is the number of monomer units in one polymer molecule. Properties depend on the molecular mass: too low and the polymer is brittle, too high and it is tough and hard to shape. A minimum DP, the critical degree, is needed for useful properties; it is lower for polymers with hydrogen bonding (nylon 6) than for polythene.","w":["Hydrogen bonding between chains lowers the critical degree of polymerisation: nylon 6 needs fewer units than polythene."]},"15.5":{"s":"Most plastics do not break down and pile up as waste. Biodegradable polymers can be broken down by microorganisms. Examples are PHBV (used in packaging, orthopaedic devices and controlled drug release) and nylon-2-nylon-6.","w":["PHBV and nylon 2-nylon 6 are biodegradable because their ester or amide links can be broken by microorganisms."]}};
